---
// Testimonial band (Task 6, Course PDP Chunk A) — Figma desktop 1106:15576
// (file FX7PDNvhZwyozODaq8Q8i7) / mobile 1109:14382 (the "Sole Switch Pro
// Page" > "Testimonial" instance inside the professional-courses mobile
// frame 1109:14374). Prop is `{ item }` (same shape as CourseOverview /
// YoullStopAndInstead / ComparisonChart), reading `item.pdp.testimonial`.
// Deliberately generic — nothing course-specific is hardcoded — so it can
// also serve the deferred Walk product PDP later without changes.
//
// `item.pdp.testimonial` = `{ quote, author, role, rating }`, this task's
// brief-mandated single-testimonial shape (an array is also accepted, see
// below, for a future carousel — but Sole Switch Pro only has one
// testimonial, so nothing here builds carousel logic). `quote` is an array
// of paragraph strings, one per `<p>` in the pulled Figma code, not a
// single string: the frame's first paragraph has a genuine, DELIBERATE
// internal line break (`<br aria-hidden />` in the pulled code) between two
// sentences — it lands on both the 916px-wide desktop card AND the 342px
// mobile card, so it isn't a width-based reflow artifact, it's real data.
// That literal break is kept as a `\n` inside the one paragraph string and
// rendered as a `<br>` below (same "deliberate break kept as data, not
// markup" precedent CourseDetails.astro's `courseCard.titleLines` set).
// `role` is part of the shape per the brief but is `null` here — Phyllis'
// attribution in both frames is name-only, no title/role line — and the
// markup only renders a role line when one is present, same optional-field
// guard every other section here uses (e.g. `item.pdp.description`).
//
// ---- Star row: NOT a reuse of ../plp/StarRating.astro -------------------
// CourseDetails.astro reuses StarRating for its own rating row; this
// component deliberately does not, for two real reasons the pulled Figma
// code confirms:
//   1. StarRating always renders a visible "(count)" chip next to the
//      stars and an sr-only "...N reviews" string. This frame shows
//      neither — just 5 plain stars, no count anywhere — so reusing it
//      as-is would add UI this design doesn't have.
//   2. StarRating fills its "on" stars with `--color-yellow` — invisible
//      against THIS section's `--color-yellow` band background. The
//      pulled code confirms the frame's own stars are flattened to a
//      single white raster, i.e. genuinely a different color per frame,
//      not an oversight.
// So the star row here is bespoke markup: 5 inline SVGs reusing
// StarRating's own star path (for visual consistency across the site) —
// the rated count filled `--color-paper` (white), the remainder
// white-outlined, so the visual tracks `rating` (and can't diverge from
// the sr-only label) exactly as StarRating's own filled/outline split
// does. A standalone visually-hidden "Rated N out of 5 stars" string
// carries the accessible label (no review-count concept applies to a
// single testimonial). The quote + attribution are wrapped in a
// figure/figcaption so the attribution is semantically tied to its quote
// (a person's name is deliberately NOT a `<cite>` — the HTML spec limits
// `<cite>` to titles of works, not people).
//
// ---- Carousel arrows: static placeholders, not a real slider ------------
// Both frames show prev/next arrow affordances (desktop: flanking the
// card; mobile: stacked below it). Per this task's brief a real slider is
// DEFERRED — there's only one testimonial in the data today, nothing to
// page between — so the arrows render as pure decoration: an
// `aria-hidden="true"` wrapper around plain, non-interactive `<span>`s,
// NOT `<button>`s. A `<button>`, even `disabled`, still promises "this is
// a control" to some assistive tech (added to a rotor/buttons list,
// announced as unavailable) — for markup that currently does nothing and
// isn't even styled to LOOK disabled (Figma renders them fully opaque, no
// dimming), that would be a false affordance. A plain hidden decorative
// element matches what sighted users see too: static artwork with no
// hover/focus treatment, same as it reads to a screen reader (nothing).
// No `cursor: pointer` either, for the same reason. When a real slider is
// built (its own task), swap these for real `<button aria-label="Previous
// testimonial">` / `aria-label="Next testimonial"` elements wired to
// prev/next state; that slider must be keyboard-operable, respect
// `prefers-reduced-motion` for any transition, and initialize via this
// project's init-on-load / `astro:after-swap` pattern (listeners attached
// once at module scope) so it doesn't leak across Astro page transitions.
// This task adds no client `<script>` at all — every element here is
// inert markup, so there is nothing to init or leak.
//
// Figma's own arrow icon is a flattened raster in the pulled code
// (`imgVector5`/`imgVector6` — no reusable vector path data), and neither
// existing repo icon in `/images/nav/` matches its thin open-stroke
// chevron shape (`arrow.svg`/`arrow-lg.svg` are solid filled triangles for
// a different UI — nav mega-menu cards / PLP CTA rows) — so a small inline
// stroke-arrow SVG is built here instead of reusing either. Figma's own
// left arrow is the SAME asset as the right arrow, just `rotate-180`'d —
// reproduced the same way: one `ARROW_PATH`, rendered twice, the `--prev`
// copy rotated 180deg in CSS rather than mirrored/duplicated.
//
// ---- Tokens / literals ---------------------------------------------------
// Yellow band = --color-yellow (#FEC745, exact hex match to Figma's
// `--bg-brand-strong`). Quote text = --color-ink on the yellow band
// (Figma's pulled code shows plain `text-black`; --color-ink is this
// repo's near-black ink token, reused rather than a raw `#000` literal,
// same substitution CourseDetails/ComparisonChart make elsewhere). Author
// name = --color-ink too (Figma's `--text-primary`, #231f20, exact hex
// match), bold. Quote type has no exact-match token (Figma shows unbound
// 16px/400-weight Montserrat) — --type-body-16 (400/16px/1.4) is the
// closest real token, reused unmodified at both breakpoints, same
// "one non-responsive token" precedent YoullStopAndInstead/ComparisonChart
// set. Author name is 16px/700 in the pulled code — no exact bold-16 token
// exists either; --type-label (600/16px) is the closest (a 100-weight
// difference, same tolerance those components already accept), reused
// rather than inventing a new literal.
//
// Section outer vertical padding: Figma's own frame is `py-96` (96px) on
// BOTH breakpoints — bigger than this repo's largest --space step
// (--space-8, 64px) — kept as a commented literal per this task's
// "literal OK if no token matches" allowance, not stretched onto the
// wrong token. The desktop card's 916px width, its --space-7 (48px) gap to
// the flanking arrows, and the ~251px-wide attribution column (identical,
// non-responsive, on both frames) are literals for the same reason —
// called out inline. The mobile arrow-pair's own internal gap has no
// exact source measurement (Figma flattened both icons into one raster
// there), so it reuses --space-5 as a reasonable approximation, flagged
// inline rather than presented as a precise pull.
const { item } = Astro.props;
// Accepts a single object (this task's real shape) or an array (a light
// future-proofing allowance the brief offers for the deferred carousel,
// not a feature built here) — always renders just the first testimonial.
const raw = item.pdp?.testimonial;
const testimonial = Array.isArray(raw) ? raw[0] : raw;
const ARROW_PATH = 'M4 12H20M20 12L14 6M20 12L14 18';
const STAR_PATH =
  'M12 .587l3.668 7.568 8.332 1.151-6.064 5.828 1.48 8.279L12 19.771l-7.416 3.642 1.48-8.279L0 9.306l8.332-1.151z';
---
{testimonial && (
  <section class="testimonial" data-pdp-id={item.id}>
    <div class="testimonial__inner">
      <div class="testimonial__row">
        <div class="testimonial__card">
          <span class="testimonial__stars" aria-hidden="true">
            {[1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map((n) => (
              <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" class={n <= testimonial.rating ? 'is-filled' : ''}>
                <path d={STAR_PATH} />
              </svg>
            ))}
          </span>
          <p class="testimonial__sr-only">{`Rated ${testimonial.rating} out of 5 stars`}</p>

          <figure class="testimonial__figure">
            <blockquote class="testimonial__quote">
              {testimonial.quote.map((paragraph) => (
                <p>
                  {paragraph.split('\n').map((line, i, lines) => (
                    <>
                      {line}
                      {i < lines.length - 1 && <br />}
                    </>
                  ))}
                </p>
              ))}
            </blockquote>
            <figcaption class="testimonial__attribution">
              <span class="testimonial__author">{testimonial.author}</span>
              {testimonial.role && <span class="testimonial__role">{testimonial.role}</span>}
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="testimonial__arrows" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="testimonial__arrow testimonial__arrow--prev">
            <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path d={ARROW_PATH} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            </svg>
          </span>
          <span class="testimonial__arrow testimonial__arrow--next">
            <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
              <path d={ARROW_PATH} stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            </svg>
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  .testimonial__inner {
    max-width: var(--container-max);
    margin-inline: auto;
    /* Mobile (1109:14382): the yellow band is edge-to-edge — zero outer
       horizontal padding, the card's own px-24 provides the text inset.
       Desktop re-adds side padding around the fixed 916px card below. */
    padding-inline: 0;
    /* 96px literal — see header comment (bigger than --space-8, the
       largest step in this repo's scale); identical on both breakpoints
       per the Figma frames, so no responsive override needed. */
    padding-block: 96px;
  }

  .testimonial__row {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    /* Card -> arrows-group gap, mobile (Figma 1109:14382 `gap-24`). */
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  /* ---- Card ------------------------------------------------------------ */
  .testimonial__card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    width: 100%;
    background: var(--color-yellow);
    text-align: center;
    /* Mobile padding (Figma 1109:14382: px-24/py-32) — both exact token
       matches. */
    padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-5);
    /* Visual order fixed to 1 explicitly (see .testimonial__row below —
       the arrows-group is `order: 2` at mobile, `display: contents` at
       desktop so its own children take the `order` that flanks the card
       there instead). */
    order: 1;
  }

  .testimonial__stars {
    display: inline-flex;
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }
  /* Fill only the rated count; outline the rest (white stroke on the yellow
     band) so the visual tracks `rating` and can't silently disagree with the
     sr-only "Rated N out of 5" label — StarRating's own filled/outline
     convention, kept generic for the Walk PDP reuse. */
  .testimonial__stars svg {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--color-paper);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
  }
  .testimonial__stars svg.is-filled {
    fill: var(--color-paper);
    stroke: none;
  }

  /* Quote + attribution are a figure/figcaption pair (associates the
     attribution with its quote); the card gap handled blockquote->attribution
     spacing before, so the figure reproduces it as its own column gap. */
  .testimonial__figure {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-6);
  }

  .testimonial__quote {
    font: var(--type-body-16);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  .testimonial__quote p + p {
    /* 8px literal == --space-2; Figma's own `mb-[8px]` between paragraphs. */
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }

  .testimonial__attribution {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    /* 251px literal — see header comment (identical, non-responsive,
       fixed-width column on both frames). */
    max-width: 251px;
  }
  .testimonial__author {
    font: var(--type-label);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  .testimonial__role {
    font: var(--type-body-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* ---- Arrows (static placeholders — see header comment) --------------- */
  .testimonial__arrows {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* Approximate — see header comment (Figma flattened both icons into
       one raster on the mobile frame, no exact measurement to pull). */
    gap: var(--space-5);
    order: 2;
  }
  .testimonial__arrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    /* Not a control yet — no pointer cursor, no hover/focus treatment
       (see header comment: matches its "nothing" a11y presentation). */
    cursor: default;
  }
  .testimonial__arrow--prev svg {
    /* Same technique as the pulled Figma code: one arrow asset, the
       "prev" copy rotated 180deg rather than mirrored/duplicated. */
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }

  .testimonial__sr-only {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  /* ---- Desktop (Figma 1106:15576, ≥1024) -------------------------------- */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .testimonial__inner {
      padding-inline: var(--space-7);
    }
    .testimonial__row {
      flex-direction: row;
      justify-content: center;
      /* Arrow<->card gap (Figma `gap-48`, exact --space-7 match). */
      gap: var(--space-7);
    }
    .testimonial__card {
      /* 916px literal — see header comment (fixed card width on the
         1200-wide desktop frame, not a fluid/full-bleed section). */
      width: 916px;
      max-width: 100%;
      /* Desktop padding (Figma 1106:15576: py-64 exact --space-8 match;
         px-96 literal — see header comment, bigger than --space-8). */
      padding: var(--space-8) 96px;
      order: 2;
    }
    /* Desktop attribution gap is gap-16 (--space-4) vs mobile's gap-12
       (--space-3) — a real per-breakpoint value (matters once a role line
       exists, e.g. the Walk PDP reuse). */
    .testimonial__attribution {
      gap: var(--space-4);
    }
    /* Dissolve the mobile arrows-group wrapper so its two children become
       direct flex items of `.testimonial__row` — letting `order` flank
       the card with them (arrow, card, arrow) the way the desktop frame
       actually lays them out, instead of the mobile "card, then a paired
       arrows row below it" grouping. The wrapper stays `aria-hidden` and
       carries no accessible content either way, so `display: contents`
       here has no a11y-semantics cost. */
    .testimonial__arrows {
      display: contents;
    }
    .testimonial__arrow--prev {
      order: 1;
    }
    .testimonial__arrow--next {
      order: 3;
    }
  }
</style>
